Z||000011||stimpy||05-15-2001||10:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>For those of you who want to blame George Bush for this mess, read Cliff's post and think about it a minute.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sheesh, talk about setting it up on the tee for someone. Of course you are going to bait someone with opposing political ideology and this will turn into another "radical vs reactionary" rant. The fact is, the oil companies are a WHOLE lot more powerful then any world leader.  It's not going to matter who is in the White House, Senate, or House of Representatives. If the oil companies feel like reaching out and grabbing a handful of short curlies, ain't nuthin that anyone can do. The fact that Dubya's cronies are getting richer is purely happy coincidence.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||deceler8||05-15-2001||10:18 AM||||Remember, gas approached $2.50 per gallon in the Midwest in summer 2000...as I recall, Bill Clinton was President then.<P>Sorry, but Bush/Cheney are telling it like it is, and no one wants to hear that. Too many folks expect the Government to somehow fix this, when it's all supply and demand. There are 48 separate gasoline formulas required by environmental laws in the US...this forces refineries to make gas in batches which is inefficient. Some of these blends require extensive refining which reduces the amount of gas that can be produced from a barrel of oil.<P>Environmental laws require all cars in a given area to use the same gas to control smog, when newer cars and properly tuned older cars are only marginally cleaner using this gas. It's the untuned older cars and "beaters" that are the real source of the air  pollution problems.<P>Seems to me that one blend of gas everywhere and cracking down on the polluting older or untuned cars would be the way to go, but this would unfairly hurt the "poor" who drive polluting clunkers. So we all pay and probably pay a lot more as a result.<P>IMO, we won't have this problem in five years. New refineries will be built, new technologies to improve fuel efficiency will arrive, and the regulatory climate will have been improved to reduce the burden of all these conflicting environmental laws.||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroJoe||05-15-2001||10:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B>Remember, gas approached $2.50 per gallon in the Midwest in summer 2000...as I recall, Bill Clinton was President then.<P>Sorry, but Bush/Cheney are telling it like it is, and no one wants to hear that. Too many folks expect the Government to somehow fix this, when it's all supply and demand. There are 48 separate gasoline formulas required by environmental laws in the US...this forces refineries to make gas in batches which is inefficient. Some of these blends require extensive refining which reduces the amount of gas that can be produced from a barrel of oil.<P>Environmental laws require all cars in a given area to use the same gas to control smog, when newer cars and properly tuned older cars are only marginally cleaner using this gas. It's the untuned older cars and "beaters" that are the real source of the air  pollution problems.<P>Seems to me that one blend of gas everywhere and cracking down on the polluting older or untuned cars would be the way to go, but this would unfairly hurt the "poor" who drive polluting clunkers. So we all pay and probably pay a lot more as a result.<P>IMO, we won't have this problem in five years. New refineries will be built, new technologies to improve fuel efficiency will arrive, and the regulatory climate will have been improved to reduce the burden of all these conflicting environmental laws.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree with all of that.  A very rational explanation, and a good one (dont hear much of those).  As for "we won't have this problem in five years."  I hope you're right!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000014||N_Man||05-15-2001||01:09 PM||||Well im gonna piss more then a few people off with this one but it has to be said.<P>The USA is NOT the center of the world.<BR>Im totally sick of hearing that Bush or clinton or whoever your president is has a serious effect on gas prices anywhere in the world.  Fact is you should stop thinking that the US is the ruler of the world because it is NOT and what your president does WILL NOT affect gas prices noticably out side of your own damn boarders.<P>I live in ALBERTA Canada and have a HUGE supply of crude oil just to the north of me but im still paying high gas prices, note Alberta is the wealthiest province in Canada because of its oil reserves.  KRMfiero is probably on the mark with needing more oil refineries.<P>Anyways gas prices are high everywhere and we are all gonna have to live with it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||87ECO5||05-15-2001||01:30 PM||||I don't think $2.00/gallon is too hateful.  Of course, that's coming from an owner of a 4 cylinder car that gets over 30 MPG.  Maybe some of those gas-guzzling big car and truck owners will decide it costs too much to run their beasts and just park them!<P>Heck, I parked my Pathfinder (an - at best - 20 MPG monster) and my 3.4 Fiero only gets driven occasionally (I could get about 30 out of her ... if I could just keep my foot out of it!!!).||||reg||1
Z||000016||Raydar||05-15-2001||05:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B> It's the untuned older cars and "beaters" that are the real source of the air  pollution problems.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I remember reading that one poorly tuned car can be responsible for making as much pollution as several hundred (or more) well tuned ones.  <P>It just grates on my nerves whenever I get behind a 70s or 80s or even newer POS that is belching blue or black smoke from it's exhaust pipe. There are laws in my state and probably every other that allow police to ticket "gross polluters". <BR>Do I ever see one get stopped? Not a chance. <BR>But I still have to pay $25 a year to make sure that each of my cars still have "fresh breath".<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||8
Z||000017||Cliff Pennock||05-15-2001||05:16 PM||||What chops my high, is that out of that $4.45, about $2.00 is tax. Our government has been raising tax on gas slowly over the past years from about $0.25 to what it is today. Everytime they raised it, they said it was temporarily but it has never once been lowered.<P>On top of that, I pay about $400 "road-tax" per year. We have about the highest gas prices in Europe but in every other country, road-tax is included in the gas price. And of course, there's the 19% sales tax.<P>A dutch economist had one time calculated that here in Holland, for every dollar we make, about 80-90 cents go back to the states in the form of taxes.<p>[This message has been edited by Cliff Pennock (edited 05-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||87ECO5||05-16-2001||07:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>A dutch economist had one time calculated that here in Holland, for every dollar we make, about 80-90 cents go back to the states in the form of taxes.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wellllll ... let's just scratch off the 'move to the Netherlands' item on my list of things to do before I die!!!||||reg||6
